values.value:
== DATATABLE & LIST VIEW GENERATION ==
Use when: the ticket needs a tabular list of records with any of sorting,
filtering, pagination, inline editing, row-level actions, or bulk
(multi-row) actions. Built on <lightning-datatable> — never hand-roll a
<table> for this; the base component already implements accessible
sorting/selection/inline-edit affordances the platform expects.

-- BASE SHAPE: columns array + data array + key-field --
  Template:
    <lightning-datatable
        key-field="Id"
        data={records}
        columns={columns}>
    </lightning-datatable>

  JS:
    columns = [
        { label: 'Name', fieldName: 'Name', type: 'text' },
        { label: 'Amount', fieldName: 'Amount', type: 'currency' },
        { label: 'Close Date', fieldName: 'CloseDate', type: 'date' }
    ];

CRITICAL RULE: key-field MUST be set, and MUST be set to the field that
ACTUALLY holds each row's unique identifier — almost always "Id" (capital
I), because that is the real API name of a Salesforce record's Id field as
it comes back from Apex/SOQL/getRecord. lightning-datatable's key-field
attribute defaults to "id" (lowercase) when omitted. Salesforce data never
has a lowercase "id" property, so omitting key-field (or leaving it
defaulted) means the datatable cannot reliably match rows to their key —
row selection, inline-edit draft tracking, and row actions all silently
misbehave with no deploy-time error, since the component still renders
rows and merely gets their identity tracking wrong.

RULES:
- fieldName in each column MUST be the record's flat field API name as it
  appears on the data array's row objects — not a relationship path like
  Account.Name (flatten relationship fields onto the row object yourself,
  e.g. row.AccountName, before handing the array to the datatable).
- type drives BOTH rendering and, for editable columns, the edit widget —
  common values: 'text', 'number', 'currency', 'date', 'email', 'phone',
  'url', 'boolean', 'action'. Never invent a type value not in this list.

-- SORTING --
lightning-datatable does NOT sort its own data automatically — setting
sortable: true on a column only makes the column HEADER clickable and
fires onsort; the component's data array must be re-sorted by YOUR code
and passed back in, and sorted-by/sorted-direction must be bound back so
the arrow indicator reflects the current state:

  Template:
    <lightning-datatable
        key-field="Id"
        data={records}
        columns={columns}
        sorted-by={sortedBy}
        sorted-direction={sortedDirection}
        onsort={handleSort}>
    </lightning-datatable>

  JS:
    sortedBy;
    sortedDirection;

    handleSort(event) {
        const { fieldName: sortedBy, sortDirection } = event.detail;
        const cloneData = [...this.records];
        cloneData.sort((a, b) => {
            const valueA = a[sortedBy] ?? '';
            const valueB = b[sortedBy] ?? '';
            return sortDirection === 'asc'
                ? (valueA > valueB ? 1 : -1)
                : (valueA > valueB ? -1 : 1);
        });
        this.records = cloneData;
        this.sortedBy = sortedBy;
        this.sortedDirection = sortDirection;
    }

RULES:
- Every column meant to be sortable needs sortable: true in its column
  definition — onsort never fires for a column missing this flag.
- handleSort MUST update BOTH this.records (the reordered data) AND
  this.sortedBy/this.sortedDirection (bound to sorted-by/sorted-direction)
  — updating only the data reorders the rows but leaves the header's sort
  arrow stuck pointing at the wrong column/direction, which reads as a
  half-broken sort to the user even though the rows did reorder correctly.

-- FILTERING --
lightning-datatable has no built-in filter UI. Pair it with a
lightning-input (search) or lightning-combobox (picklist filter) elsewhere
on the component, and filter the SOURCE data array in JS — never filter by
mutating the array bound to the datatable's own data attribute in place;
reassign a new filtered array so the framework's reactivity picks it up:

  handleSearchChange(event) {
      const searchTerm = event.target.value.toLowerCase();
      this.filteredRecords = this.allRecords.filter((record) =>
          record.Name?.toLowerCase().includes(searchTerm)
      );
  }

RULE: keep the FULL unfiltered list in one property (e.g. allRecords,
populated once from @wire/Apex) and bind the datatable to a SEPARATE
filtered property (e.g. filteredRecords) — filtering the same array the
wire populated means the original data is lost the moment a filter is
applied, so clearing the filter can no longer restore the full list.

-- PAGINATION --
lightning-datatable has no automatic page-by-page pagination either.
Two supported approaches — pick based on what the ticket implies:

  (1) CLIENT-SIDE (all records already loaded, e.g. from one Apex/SOQL
      call): slice the full array in JS and drive it off Previous/Next
      buttons.
        get pagedRecords() {
            const start = this.pageNumber * this.pageSize;
            return this.filteredRecords.slice(start, start + this.pageSize);
        }
        get isLastPage() {
            return (this.pageNumber + 1) * this.pageSize >= this.filteredRecords.length;
        }
        handleNext() {
            if (!this.isLastPage) { this.pageNumber += 1; }
        }
        handlePrevious() {
            if (this.pageNumber > 0) { this.pageNumber -= 1; }
        }

      To show a human-readable "Page X of Y" indicator, ALWAYS expose it
      as a GETTER — never write the arithmetic directly inside a {...}
      template binding (e.g. {pageNumber + 1} is INVALID LWC: a binding
      may only be a bare property/getter reference, never an inline
      expression). This is a real, confirmed failure mode — 2026-08-26,
      opportunityPipelineManager — generation failed static validation
      twice in a row because the LLM wrote {pageNumber + 1} directly in
      the template with no getter to fall back on:
        get currentPageDisplay() {
            return this.pageNumber + 1; // convert 0-indexed to 1-indexed for display
        }
        get totalPages() {
            return Math.ceil(this.filteredRecords.length / this.pageSize) || 1;
        }
      Template: <p>Page {currentPageDisplay} of {totalPages}</p>

  (2) INFINITE SCROLL / SERVER-DRIVEN PAGING (a large or unbounded record
      set, fetched a page at a time): use enable-infinite-loading +
      onloadmore, and set is-loading to reflect an in-flight fetch:
        <lightning-datatable
            key-field="Id"
            data={records}
            columns={columns}
            enable-infinite-loading
            onloadmore={handleLoadMore}
            is-loading={isLoading}>
        </lightning-datatable>

        async handleLoadMore(event) {
            event.target.isLoading = true;
            const newRecords = await fetchNextPage({ offset: this.records.length });
            this.records = [...this.records, ...newRecords];
            event.target.isLoading = false;
            if (newRecords.length === 0) {
                event.target.enableInfiniteLoading = false;
            }
        }

RULE: never invent a "page-size"/"current-page" attribute on
<lightning-datatable> itself — it does not exist; pagination is always
built in your OWN component state using one of the two approaches above.

-- INLINE EDIT --
A column becomes editable with editable: true in its definition; the
datatable itself needs an onsave handler to receive the user's edits and a
draft-values attribute bound back to a reactive property:

  Template:
    <lightning-datatable
        key-field="Id"
        data={records}
        columns={columns}
        draft-values={draftValues}
        onsave={handleSave}>
    </lightning-datatable>

  JS:
    columns = [
        { label: 'Name', fieldName: 'Name', type: 'text', editable: true },
        { label: 'Amount', fieldName: 'Amount', type: 'currency', editable: true }
    ];
    draftValues = [];

    async handleSave(event) {
        const updates = event.detail.draftValues.map((draft) =>
            updateRecord({ fields: { ...draft } })
        );
        try {
            await Promise.all(updates);
            this.dispatchEvent(new ShowToastEvent({
                title: 'Success',
                message: 'Records updated.',
                variant: 'success'
            }));
            await refreshApex(this.wiredRecordsResult);
        } catch (error) {
            this.dispatchEvent(new ShowToastEvent({
                title: 'Error updating records',
                message: getErrorMessage(error),
                variant: 'error'
            }));
        } finally {
            // MANDATORY — see CRITICAL RULE below.
            this.draftValues = [];
        }
    }

CRITICAL RULE: draftValues MUST be reset back to an empty array
(this.draftValues = [];) once the save completes — in the finally
block/branch so it resets on BOTH success and failure. Skipping this
leaves the datatable permanently in its "editing" state: the edited
cells stay visually highlighted and the Save/Cancel action bar never
disappears, even though the write itself succeeded — a real, well-known
lightning-datatable footgun, not a hypothetical one.

RULES:
- event.detail.draftValues is an array of PARTIAL row objects — each one
  contains ONLY the fields the user actually changed, PLUS the row's
  key-field value (Id) so updateRecord knows which record to write. Never
  assume every column's field is present in a given draft entry.
- Prefer Promise.all over a sequential loop when saving multiple
  draftValues — each row is an independent LDS write, so there is no
  ordering dependency between them.
- Combine with LWC_LDS's guidance on updateRecord (fields must include Id
  — already guaranteed here since draftValues always carries the row's
  key-field value) and LWC_NAVIGATION's ShowToastEvent/getErrorMessage
  conventions for the success/error feedback.
- If the ticket's save action is a custom Apex method instead of LDS
  updateRecord (e.g. the edit needs validation logic beyond a plain field
  write), the same draftValues-reset-in-finally requirement still applies
  — it is a datatable-UI-state rule, independent of which write mechanism
  the onsave handler ultimately calls.

-- ROW ACTIONS (per-row menu: Edit / Delete / View / custom actions) --
Add a column with type: 'action', and handle the single onrowaction event
the datatable dispatches for ANY row's action menu selection — never wire
a separate handler per action, there is exactly one event for all of them,
disambiguated by event.detail.action.name:

  Template:
    <lightning-datatable
        key-field="Id"
        data={records}
        columns={columns}
        onrowaction={handleRowAction}>
    </lightning-datatable>

  JS:
    columns = [
        { label: 'Name', fieldName: 'Name', type: 'text' },
        {
            type: 'action',
            typeAttributes: {
                rowActions: [
                    { label: 'View', name: 'view' },
                    { label: 'Edit', name: 'edit' },
                    { label: 'Delete', name: 'delete' }
                ]
            }
        }
    ];

    handleRowAction(event) {
        const actionName = event.detail.action.name;
        const row = event.detail.row;
        switch (actionName) {
            case 'view':
                this.handleView(row);
                break;
            case 'edit':
                this.handleEdit(row);
                break;
            case 'delete':
                this.handleDelete(row);
                break;
            default:
                break;
        }
    }

CRITICAL RULE: a column with type: 'action' with NO onrowaction handler on
the <lightning-datatable> element renders a perfectly normal-looking
action dropdown that does absolutely nothing when a user picks an item —
no error, no console warning, the menu just closes. Every action column
MUST be paired with an onrowaction handler on the same element.

RULES:
- event.detail.action.name matches the name you gave that action in
  rowActions — never event.detail.action.label (the label is display text
  only, and can be changed independently of the name used for dispatch
  logic).
- event.detail.row is the FULL row data object for the row the action was
  taken on — use it directly rather than re-deriving the row from a
  separately-tracked "selected row" property.
- A destructive action (delete) taken from a row action MUST still show
  ShowToastEvent feedback per LWC_NAVIGATION's mandatory-feedback rule —
  row actions are user-triggered writes exactly like a standalone button.

-- BULK / MULTI-ROW SELECTION AND ACTIONS --
Enable checkboxes with show-row-number-column or (more commonly for bulk
actions) leaving selection on by default, read the current selection via
onrowselection, and drive any bulk-action buttons off that array's length:

  Template:
    <lightning-datatable
        key-field="Id"
        data={records}
        columns={columns}
        onrowselection={handleRowSelection}>
    </lightning-datatable>
    <lightning-button
        label="Delete Selected"
        disabled={noRowsSelected}
        onclick={handleBulkDelete}>
    </lightning-button>

  JS:
    selectedRows = [];

    get noRowsSelected() {
        return this.selectedRows.length === 0;
    }

    handleRowSelection(event) {
        this.selectedRows = event.detail.selectedRows;
    }

    async handleBulkDelete() {
        this.isLoading = true;
        try {
            await Promise.all(
                this.selectedRows.map((row) => deleteRecord(row.Id))
            );
            // ... toast success, refreshApex ...
        } catch (error) {
            // ... toast error via getErrorMessage(error) ...
        } finally {
            this.isLoading = false;
        }
    }

RULES:
- Bulk-action buttons are disabled (or hidden) whenever selectedRows is
  empty — never let a user click "Delete Selected" with nothing selected.
- A bulk write is still ONE user-triggered action for feedback purposes —
  ONE ShowToastEvent summarizing the whole batch's outcome, not one toast
  per row.
- max-row-selection={n} on <lightning-datatable> caps how many rows can be
  checked at once — use it only when the ticket explicitly limits bulk
  selection size; omit it for an uncapped bulk action.

== NEVER ==
- Never omit key-field on <lightning-datatable> — it defaults to "id"
  (lowercase), which never matches a real Salesforce record's "Id" field.
- Never assume lightning-datatable sorts its own data — sortable: true only
  fires onsort; your code must re-sort the array and rebind sorted-by/
  sorted-direction yourself.
- Never invent a page-size/current-page attribute on lightning-datatable —
  pagination is always custom component state (client-side slicing or
  enable-infinite-loading + onloadmore), never a built-in prop.
- Never write pagination arithmetic directly inside a template binding
  (e.g. {pageNumber + 1}) — a {...} binding may only be a bare property or
  getter reference. Expose a currentPageDisplay-style getter instead.
- Never leave draftValues unreset after onsave completes — always reset it
  to [] in a path that runs on both success and failure (a finally block).
- Never add a type: 'action' column without an onrowaction handler on the
  same <lightning-datatable> element.
- Never filter a datatable's bound data array by mutating it in place —
  keep the full source list separate from the filtered/displayed list.
